IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hprnlq-dates`

-- Health Practitioner Regulation National Law Act 2009 (Qld) -- the host of the Health Practitioner Regulation National
-- Law. The Act's own provisions (ss 1-10) adopt the Law set out in its schedule, as modified by its Part 4, as the
-- Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland) (s 4); declare Queensland a co-regulatory jurisdiction with
-- the health ombudsman as co-regulatory authority and adjudication body (ss 7A-7C); make QCAT the responsible tribunal
-- (s 6) and an appeal to QCAT a review under the QCAT Act (s 9); and shut nine Queensland Acts out of the Law (s 7).
-- Part 4 (ss 11-59) then rewrites Part 8 of the Law so that complaints go to the health ombudsman, not to Ahpra.
--
-- This encoding takes the provisions a person harmed by, or worried about, a registered health practitioner in
-- Queensland meets: whether a Queensland Act reaches the Law (s 7); who a complaint can be about (ss 138-139B with the
-- Queensland (1A) and (2A)); where and on what grounds to complain (ss 143A-146); who was required to notify the health
-- ombudsman (ss 139D-143); what happens when the health ombudsman refers the complaint to the National Board (ss 148-151:
-- the 60-day preliminary assessment, no further action); what the complainant must or may be told later (ss 159A,
-- 161(3), 167A, 177A, 180, 187, 189, 192); when the Board must bring in the health ombudsman, and which tribunal hears a
-- referral (ss 193-193C, with ss 6 and 9 of the Act); professional indemnity insurance (s 129); and the complainant's
-- protections (s 237, and the omission of ss 237A and 237B by s 56AA of the Act).
--
-- SEAT: the complainant -- usually a patient, or a patient's family member, harmed by or worried about a registered
-- health practitioner's care or conduct in Queensland, who may also be bringing or settling a negligence claim. "Where
-- do I complain about my surgeon? Did the hospital have to report her? By when must the Board look at it once the
-- health ombudsman sends it on? Will I be told? Am I protected?" Every judgment the Law gives a National Board, a panel,
-- the health ombudsman or a court ("reasonably believes", "is satisfied", "in good faith", "not in the public interest")
-- is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: Health Practitioner Regulation National Law Act 2009 (Qld), in force from 10 April 2026, and the Health
-- Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland) as published by Queensland Legislation, in force from 10 April 2026,
-- both retrieved 6 November 2026 and deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-2009-045.txt and act-2009-hprnlq.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: see NOTES.md (Scope). The Health Ombudsman Act 2013, which now governs most of a complaint's life, is
-- named in the answers but not encoded here.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the complainant's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland) -- the complainant`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `section 7 of the Act does not list this Act; whether it applies to the National Law (Queensland) turns on that Act and the general law, not encoded`
    `Part 8 speaks of a person registered under this Law, or formerly registered, or registered under a corresponding prior Act; a person never registered is dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 and Part 8 Division 7A (interim prohibition orders), not encoded`
    `section 139A reaches a former practitioner's behaviour only while the person was registered; this Law does not say what may be done about behaviour after registration ended`
    `section 144 does not list the matter you describe as a ground; you may still be able to make a health service complaint under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, not encoded`
    `this Law does not say; Division 2 of Part 8 puts the duty to notify only on registered health practitioners, employers and education providers`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `s 7 of the Act -- which Queensland Acts reach the National Law (Queensland)`

-- s 7: "The following Acts of this jurisdiction do not apply to the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law
-- (Queensland) or to the instruments made under that law -- (a) the Acts Interpretation Act 1954; (b) the
-- Auditor-General Act 2009; (c) the Financial Accountability Act 2009; (d) the Information Privacy Act 2009; (e) the
-- Ombudsman Act 2001; (f) the Public Sector Act 2022; (g) the Right to Information Act 2009; (h) the Statutory Bodies
-- Financial Arrangements Act 1982; (i) the Statutory Instruments Act 1992."
-- National Law (Queensland) s 146(1): "The Health Ombudsman Act 2013 applies to a notification (including the making of
-- a notification) under division 2 or 3". s 9 of the Act: "A reference in the Health Practitioner Regulation National
-- Law (Queensland) to an appeal against a decision is, for an appeal to QCAT as the responsible tribunal, a reference to
-- a review of the decision as provided under the QCAT Act."
DECLARE `Queensland Act` IS ONE OF
    `the Acts Interpretation Act 1954`
    `the Auditor-General Act 2009`
    `the Financial Accountability Act 2009`
    `the Information Privacy Act 2009`
    `the Ombudsman Act 2001`
    `the Public Sector Act 2022`
    `the Right to Information Act 2009`
    `the Statutory Bodies Financial Arrangements Act 1982`
    `the Statutory Instruments Act 1992`
    `the Health Ombudsman Act 2013`
    `the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2009 (the QCAT Act)`
    `another Queensland Act`

DECLARE `Does the Act reach the Law` IS ONE OF
    `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`
    `yes: the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 applies to a notification (complaint) under Part 8 Divisions 2 and 3, and the health ombudsman deals with it as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (National Law (Queensland) s 146)`
    `yes, for appeals: an appeal to QCAT under the National Law (Queensland) is a review of the decision under the QCAT Act (s 9 of the Act; QCAT is the responsible tribunal, s 6)`

@export Does this Queensland Act apply to the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland) (for example, can I use Queensland privacy or right to information law against Ahpra or a National Board)?
GIVEN `the Act` IS A `Queensland Act` @desc The Queensland Act you want to rely on, or "another Queensland Act" if it is not listed
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Does the Act reach the Law`
DECIDE `Does this Queensland Act apply to the National Law question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the Act` EQUALS `the Health Ombudsman Act 2013`
            THEN `answered` `yes: the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 applies to a notification (complaint) under Part 8 Divisions 2 and 3, and the health ombudsman deals with it as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (National Law (Queensland) s 146)`
        IF `the Act` EQUALS `the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2009 (the QCAT Act)`
            THEN `answered` `yes, for appeals: an appeal to QCAT under the National Law (Queensland) is a review of the decision under the QCAT Act (s 9 of the Act; QCAT is the responsible tribunal, s 6)`
        IF `the Act` EQUALS `another Queensland Act`
            THEN `not covered` `section 7 of the Act does not list this Act; whether it applies to the National Law (Queensland) turns on that Act and the general law, not encoded`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: section 7 of the Act says this Act does not apply to the National Law (Queensland) or to the instruments made under it`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 138-139B -- can a complaint be made about this person`

-- s 5: "registered health practitioner means an individual who -- (a) is registered under this Law to practise a health
-- profession, other than as a student; or (b) holds non-practising registration under this Law in a health profession";
-- "student means a person whose name is entered in a student register as being currently registered under this Law".
-- A "corresponding prior Act" is the State or Territory registration Act this Law replaced (s 5; borrowed, asked).
DECLARE `The person's registration` IS ONE OF
    `registered now under this Law (general, specialist, provisional, limited or non-practising registration)`
    `registered now as a student`
    `registered under this Law before, but not now`
    `registered only under a corresponding prior Act (before this Law), never under this Law`
    `never registered as a health practitioner`

-- s 138(1): "occurs while the practitioner is registered ... (b) occurred before the practitioner was registered ...
-- (c) occurred during any other period in which the practitioner was not registered".
DECLARE `When the behaviour happened` IS ONE OF
    `while the person was registered`
    `before the person was first registered`
    `during a gap in registration (for example after it ended, lapsed, or was suspended or cancelled)`

DECLARE `Can a complaint be made about them` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a notification may be made, and a matter the health ombudsman refers may be dealt with, about a registered health practitioner for behaviour while registered (s 138(1)(a), (1A))`
    `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, for behaviour before registration (s 138(1)(b), (1A)), but that behaviour cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance or unprofessional conduct (s 138(2))`
    `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, for behaviour in a gap in registration (s 138(1)(c), (1A)); it cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance, nor unprofessional conduct except where s 139 applies (practising on after registration ended under s 108(2)(a)) (s 138(2))`
    `yes: a notification may be made about a registered student, on the grounds in s 144(2)`
    `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, about a former practitioner for behaviour while registered, as if still registered (s 139A(2), (2A)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 6 (s 139A(3))`
    `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, about a person registered under a corresponding prior Act for behaviour while so registered, to the extent one could have been made under that Act (s 139B(2), (2A), (3)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 7 (s 139B(4))`
    `no: for a person registered only under a corresponding prior Act, s 139B(2) and (2A) apply only to the extent a notification could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act (s 139B(3))`

@export Can a complaint be made under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland) about this health practitioner, given their registration and when the behaviour happened?
GIVEN `their registration` IS A `The person's registration` @desc The practitioner's registration status today, as the public register or the practitioner shows it
      `when it happened` IS A `When the behaviour happened` @desc When the behaviour you are concerned about happened, compared with the practitioner's registration
      `the prior Act allowed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a person registered under a corresponding prior Act (a State or Territory registration Act before this Law): whether a complaint about the behaviour could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can a complaint be made about them`
DECIDE `Can a complaint be made about this person question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `registered now as a student`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made about a registered student, on the grounds in s 144(2)`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `never registered as a health practitioner`
            THEN `not covered` `Part 8 speaks of a person registered under this Law, or formerly registered, or registered under a corresponding prior Act; a person never registered is dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 and Part 8 Division 7A (interim prohibition orders), not encoded`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `registered now under this Law (general, specialist, provisional, limited or non-practising registration)`
            THEN `the s 138 answer` `when it happened`
        IF NOT (`when it happened` EQUALS `while the person was registered`)
            THEN `not covered` `section 139A reaches a former practitioner's behaviour only while the person was registered; this Law does not say what may be done about behaviour after registration ended`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `registered under this Law before, but not now`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, about a former practitioner for behaviour while registered, as if still registered (s 139A(2), (2A)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 6 (s 139A(3))`
        IF `the prior Act allowed it`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, about a person registered under a corresponding prior Act for behaviour while so registered, to the extent one could have been made under that Act (s 139B(2), (2A), (3)); Part 8 applies except Divisions 2 and 7 (s 139B(4))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: for a person registered only under a corresponding prior Act, s 139B(2) and (2A) apply only to the extent a notification could have been made, and proceedings taken, under that Act (s 139B(3))`

-- s 138(1), (1A), (2).
GIVEN w IS A `When the behaviour happened`
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can a complaint be made about them`
`the s 138 answer` w MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF w EQUALS `while the person was registered`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a matter the health ombudsman refers may be dealt with, about a registered health practitioner for behaviour while registered (s 138(1)(a), (1A))`
        IF w EQUALS `before the person was first registered`
            THEN `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, for behaviour before registration (s 138(1)(b), (1A)), but that behaviour cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance or unprofessional conduct (s 138(2))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `yes: a notification may be made, and a referred matter dealt with, for behaviour in a gap in registration (s 138(1)(c), (1A)); it cannot be unsatisfactory professional performance, nor unprofessional conduct except where s 139 applies (practising on after registration ended under s 108(2)(a)) (s 138(2))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 143A-146 -- where I complain, and on what grounds`

-- s 143A(1): "This division applies in relation to a registered health practitioner only if the notification concerns
-- conduct occurring in this jurisdiction or the practitioner's principal place of practice is in this jurisdiction."
-- (2): "... in relation to a student only if the notification concerns conduct occurring in this jurisdiction or the
-- student is enrolled in a program of study in this jurisdiction or is undertaking clinical training in this
-- jurisdiction." s 145: "Any entity that believes that a ground on which a voluntary notification may be made exists in
-- relation to a registered health practitioner or a student may notify the health ombudsman." s 146(2): "If a person
-- makes a notification to the health ombudsman under division 2 or 3, the health ombudsman must deal with it under the
-- Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as if it were a complaint made under part 3, division 2 of that Act."
-- s 5 of the Act: in the National Law (Queensland) "this jurisdiction means Queensland".
DECLARE `Who the notification is about` IS ONE OF
    `a registered health practitioner`
    `a registered student`

DECLARE `Can I notify` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may notify the health ombudsman, because you believe a ground in s 144(1) exists (ss 144(1), 145); the health ombudsman must deal with it under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (s 146(2))`
    `yes: you may notify the health ombudsman about the student, because you believe a ground in s 144(2) exists (ss 144(2), 145); the health ombudsman must deal with it under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (s 146(2))`
    `no: in Queensland, Division 3 applies to a practitioner only if the notification concerns conduct in Queensland or the practitioner's principal place of practice is in Queensland (s 143A(1)); notify under the National Law as it applies where the conduct happened`
    `no: in Queensland, Division 3 applies to a student only if the notification concerns conduct in Queensland or the student studies or trains in Queensland (s 143A(2)); notify under the National Law as it applies where the conduct happened`

-- s 144(2)(a): "the student has been charged with an offence, or has been convicted or found guilty of an offence, that
-- is punishable by 12 months imprisonment or more".
GIVEN `charged or convicted` IS A BOOLEAN, `maximum months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 144(2)(a) ground holds` `charged or convicted` `maximum months` IF
    `charged or convicted` AND `maximum months` AT LEAST 12

@export Can I make a voluntary notification (complaint) about a registered health practitioner or student to the Queensland health ombudsman under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `about` IS A `Who the notification is about` @desc Whether your concern is about a registered health practitioner or about a registered student
      `it happened in Queensland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct you are concerned about happened in Queensland
      `they are based in Queensland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: whether the practitioner's principal place of practice (the address they declared as where they mainly practise) is in Queensland. Student: whether the student is enrolled in a program of study, or undertaking clinical training, in Queensland
      `conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe their professional conduct is, or may be, below what the public or their professional peers might reasonably expect (s 144(1)(a))
      `care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe the knowledge, skill or judgment they have, or the care they took, is or may be below the standard reasonably expected (s 144(1)(b))
      `unsuitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe they are not, or may not be, a suitable (fit and proper) person to be registered (s 144(1)(c))
      `impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner or student: you believe they have, or may have, a physical or mental impairment, disability, condition or disorder (including substance abuse) that affects, or is likely to affect, their capacity to practise or train (s 144(1)(d), (2)(b))
      `contravened the Law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe they have, or may have, broken the National Law (for example practising without professional indemnity insurance) (s 144(1)(e))
      `contravened a condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner or student: you believe they have, or may have, broken a condition of their registration or an undertaking given to their Board (s 144(1)(f), (2)(c))
      `improperly registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Practitioner: you believe their registration was, or may have been, obtained with false or misleading information or documents (s 144(1)(g))
      `charged or convicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Student: the student has been charged with, convicted of, or found guilty of an offence
      `maximum months` IS A NUMBER @desc Student: the longest prison term, in months, that offence can be punished by (0 if none)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I notify`
DECIDE `Can I make a voluntary notification question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`it happened in Queensland` OR `they are based in Queensland`)) AND `about` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner`
            THEN `answered` `no: in Queensland, Division 3 applies to a practitioner only if the notification concerns conduct in Queensland or the practitioner's principal place of practice is in Queensland (s 143A(1)); notify under the National Law as it applies where the conduct happened`
        IF NOT (`it happened in Queensland` OR `they are based in Queensland`)
            THEN `answered` `no: in Queensland, Division 3 applies to a student only if the notification concerns conduct in Queensland or the student studies or trains in Queensland (s 143A(2)); notify under the National Law as it applies where the conduct happened`
        IF `about` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner` AND `a s 144(1) ground is believed` `conduct` `care` `unsuitable` `impairment` `contravened the Law` `contravened a condition` `improperly registered`
            THEN `answered` `yes: you may notify the health ombudsman, because you believe a ground in s 144(1) exists (ss 144(1), 145); the health ombudsman must deal with it under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (s 146(2))`
        IF `about` EQUALS `a registered student` AND ((`the s 144(2)(a) ground holds` `charged or convicted` `maximum months`) OR `impairment` OR `contravened a condition`)
            THEN `answered` `yes: you may notify the health ombudsman about the student, because you believe a ground in s 144(2) exists (ss 144(2), 145); the health ombudsman must deal with it under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 as a complaint under part 3, division 2 of that Act (s 146(2))`
        OTHERWISE `not covered` `section 144 does not list the matter you describe as a ground; you may still be able to make a health service complaint under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, not encoded`

-- s 144(1)(a)-(g).
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN, c IS A BOOLEAN, d IS A BOOLEAN, e IS A BOOLEAN, f IS A BOOLEAN, g IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a s 144(1) ground is believed` a b c d e f g IF
    a OR b OR c OR d OR e OR f OR g

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 139D-142 -- was someone required to notify the health ombudsman about the practitioner`
-- Arises only where another practitioner or the practitioner's employer knew of the conduct.

-- s 140: "notifiable conduct, in relation to a registered health practitioner, means -- (a) practising the
-- practitioner's profession while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs; or (b) engaging in sexual misconduct in connection
-- with the practice of the practitioner's profession; or (c) placing the public at risk of substantial harm in the
-- practitioner's practice of the profession because the practitioner has an impairment; or (d) placing the public at
-- risk of harm by practising the profession in a way that constitutes a significant departure from accepted
-- professional standards."
DECLARE `What they believed` IS ONE OF
    `the practitioner practised while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs`
    `the practitioner engaged, or is engaging, in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice`
    `the practitioner is only at risk of engaging in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice`
    `the practitioner practised while they had an impairment`
    `the practitioner practised in a way that was a significant departure from accepted professional standards`
    `none of these (for example a single poor outcome, rudeness, or a billing dispute)`

DECLARE `Who knew` IS ONE OF
    `a registered health practitioner who was not treating the practitioner (for example a colleague)`
    `a registered health practitioner who was providing a health service to the practitioner (their treating practitioner)`
    `the practitioner's employer (including an entity engaging them under a contract for services)`
    `someone else (for example a patient, a relative, or a manager who is not the employer)`

DECLARE `Was someone required to notify` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the practitioner must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable after forming the reasonable belief (s 141(2)); a failure is not an offence but may be behaviour for which action may be taken under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141(3))`
    `yes: the treating practitioner must notify the health ombudsman of the sexual misconduct (engaged in, engaging in, or at risk of) as soon as practicable (s 141A(2)); a failure is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141A(3))`
    `yes: the treating practitioner must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable, because the practitioner is placing the public at substantial risk of harm (s 141B(1), (2)); a failure is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141B(6))`
    `yes: the employer must notify the health ombudsman of the notifiable conduct (s 142(1)); if it does not, the health ombudsman must tell the National Agency and may refer the employer to its licensing authority or another entity, or advise the Minister (s 142(2))`
    `no: in Queensland, Division 2 applies to a practitioner's notifiable conduct only if the conduct occurs in Queensland (s 139D(1))`
    `no: the duty arises only when a reasonable belief is formed (ss 141(1), 141A(1), 141B(1), 142(1))`
    `no: what was believed is not notifiable conduct as s 140 defines it`
    `no: s 141 applies only to a belief formed in the course of practising the profession (s 141(1))`
    `no: the belief is taken not to have been formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)) or of providing a health service (s 141C(2)), for example because it was formed giving legal advice, for an indemnity insurer, on a quality assurance committee, or knowing the health ombudsman had already been told`
    `no: a treating practitioner is not required to notify under s 141 (s 141(2A)); under ss 141A and 141B a treating practitioner must notify only sexual misconduct (s 141A) or a substantial risk of harm to the public (s 141B)`

-- s 140: notifiable conduct from what was believed and the risk.
GIVEN what IS A `What they believed`, `risk of harm` IS A BOOLEAN, substantial IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is notifiable conduct` what `risk of harm` substantial IF
    BRANCH
        IF what EQUALS `the practitioner practised while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs` THEN TRUE
        IF what EQUALS `the practitioner engaged, or is engaging, in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice` THEN TRUE
        IF what EQUALS `the practitioner practised while they had an impairment` THEN `risk of harm` AND substantial
        IF what EQUALS `the practitioner practised in a way that was a significant departure from accepted professional standards` THEN `risk of harm`
        OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 141B(1): "placing the public at substantial risk of harm by practising the profession -- (a) while the
-- practitioner has an impairment; or (b) while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs; or (c) in a way that constitutes a
-- significant departure from accepted professional standards."
GIVEN what IS A `What they believed`, `risk of harm` IS A BOOLEAN, substantial IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 141B ground holds` what `risk of harm` substantial IF
        `risk of harm` AND substantial
    AND (    what EQUALS `the practitioner practised while they had an impairment`
          OR what EQUALS `the practitioner practised while intoxicated by alcohol or drugs`
          OR what EQUALS `the practitioner practised in a way that was a significant departure from accepted professional standards`)

-- s 141A(1): "has engaged, is engaging, or is at risk of engaging, in sexual misconduct in connection with the practice
-- of the practitioner's profession".
GIVEN what IS A `What they believed`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 141A ground holds` what IF
       what EQUALS `the practitioner engaged, or is engaging, in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice`
    OR what EQUALS `the practitioner is only at risk of engaging in sexual misconduct in connection with their practice`

@export Was another practitioner, or the practitioner's employer, required to notify the Queensland health ombudsman about the practitioner's conduct under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `who knew` IS A `Who knew` @desc Who knew of, or suspected, the conduct
      `it happened in Queensland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct happened in Queensland
      `what they believed` IS A `What they believed` @desc What that person believed the practitioner had done
      `risk of harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the belief was that the practitioner placed the public at risk of harm
      `the risk was substantial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the belief was that the risk of harm to the public was substantial
      `they formed a reasonable belief` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person formed a reasonable belief (not just a suspicion) of the conduct; for an employer, whether it reasonably believed it
      `in the course of practising` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a practitioner who was not treating: whether they formed the belief in the course of practising their own profession
      `an exception applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a practitioner: whether they formed the belief while working for the practitioner's indemnity insurer from a disclosure in a legal proceeding or legal advice, while advising for a legal proceeding or legal advice, as a lawyer acting for the practitioner, on a quality assurance committee whose Act forbids disclosure, or knowing or reasonably believing the health ombudsman had already been told
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Was someone required to notify`
DECIDE `Was someone required to notify question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `someone else (for example a patient, a relative, or a manager who is not the employer)`
            THEN `not covered` `this Law does not say; Division 2 of Part 8 puts the duty to notify only on registered health practitioners, employers and education providers`
        IF NOT (`it happened in Queensland`)
            THEN `answered` `no: in Queensland, Division 2 applies to a practitioner's notifiable conduct only if the conduct occurs in Queensland (s 139D(1))`
        IF NOT (`they formed a reasonable belief`)
            THEN `answered` `no: the duty arises only when a reasonable belief is formed (ss 141(1), 141A(1), 141B(1), 142(1))`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `the practitioner's employer (including an entity engaging them under a contract for services)`
            THEN `the s 142 answer` `what they believed` `risk of harm` `the risk was substantial`
        IF `who knew` EQUALS `a registered health practitioner who was providing a health service to the practitioner (their treating practitioner)`
            THEN `the treating practitioner's answer` `what they believed` `risk of harm` `the risk was substantial` `an exception applies`
        OTHERWISE `the s 141 answer` `what they believed` `risk of harm` `the risk was substantial` `in the course of practising` `an exception applies`

-- s 142(1): "If an employer of a registered health practitioner reasonably believes the health practitioner has behaved
-- in a way that constitutes notifiable conduct, the employer must notify the health ombudsman of the notifiable conduct."
-- The s 141(4) and s 141C(2) exceptions do not reach an employer.
GIVEN what IS A `What they believed`, `risk of harm` IS A BOOLEAN, substantial IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Was someone required to notify`
`the s 142 answer` what `risk of harm` substantial MEANS
    IF `it is notifiable conduct` what `risk of harm` substantial
    THEN `answered` `yes: the employer must notify the health ombudsman of the notifiable conduct (s 142(1)); if it does not, the health ombudsman must tell the National Agency and may refer the employer to its licensing authority or another entity, or advise the Minister (s 142(2))`
    ELSE `answered` `no: what was believed is not notifiable conduct as s 140 defines it`

-- s 141(1), (2), (4): a practitioner who is not treating the second practitioner.
GIVEN what IS A `What they believed`, `risk of harm` IS A BOOLEAN, substantial IS A BOOLEAN, practising IS A BOOLEAN, exception IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Was someone required to notify`
`the s 141 answer` what `risk of harm` substantial practising exception MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (practising)
            THEN `answered` `no: s 141 applies only to a belief formed in the course of practising the profession (s 141(1))`
        IF exception
            THEN `answered` `no: the belief is taken not to have been formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)) or of providing a health service (s 141C(2)), for example because it was formed giving legal advice, for an indemnity insurer, on a quality assurance committee, or knowing the health ombudsman had already been told`
        IF `it is notifiable conduct` what `risk of harm` substantial
            THEN `answered` `yes: the practitioner must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable after forming the reasonable belief (s 141(2)); a failure is not an offence but may be behaviour for which action may be taken under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141(3))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: what was believed is not notifiable conduct as s 140 defines it`

-- s 141(2A): "However, subsection (2) does not apply if the first health practitioner forms the reasonable belief in the
-- course of providing a health service to the second health practitioner or student." ss 141A, 141B, 141C.
GIVEN what IS A `What they believed`, `risk of harm` IS A BOOLEAN, substantial IS A BOOLEAN, exception IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Was someone required to notify`
`the treating practitioner's answer` what `risk of harm` substantial exception MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF exception
            THEN `answered` `no: the belief is taken not to have been formed in the course of practising (s 141(4)) or of providing a health service (s 141C(2)), for example because it was formed giving legal advice, for an indemnity insurer, on a quality assurance committee, or knowing the health ombudsman had already been told`
        IF `the s 141A ground holds` what
            THEN `answered` `yes: the treating practitioner must notify the health ombudsman of the sexual misconduct (engaged in, engaging in, or at risk of) as soon as practicable (s 141A(2)); a failure is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141A(3))`
        IF `the s 141B ground holds` what `risk of harm` substantial
            THEN `answered` `yes: the treating practitioner must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable, because the practitioner is placing the public at substantial risk of harm (s 141B(1), (2)); a failure is not an offence but may lead to action under Part 8 or the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (s 141B(6))`
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: a treating practitioner is not required to notify under s 141 (s 141(2A)); under ss 141A and 141B a treating practitioner must notify only sexual misconduct (s 141A) or a substantial risk of harm to the public (s 141B)`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 148-151 -- when the health ombudsman refers my complaint to the National Board`
-- Arises only if the health ombudsman refers the matter to the National Agency (Ahpra) under the Health Ombudsman Act
-- 2013, part 9, division 1 (s 148).

-- s 149: "The National Agency must immediately refer the matter to the National Board established for the health
-- practitioner's or student's profession." s 150(1): "A National Board must, within 60 days after receipt of a referred
-- matter under section 148, conduct a preliminary assessment of the referred matter and decide -- (a) whether or not the
-- referred matter relates to a person who is a health practitioner or a student registered in a health profession for
-- which the Board is established; and (b) whether or not the referred matter relates to a matter that is a ground for
-- notification." The 60 days are reckoned by Schedule 7 cl 31(2), (3) (see hprnlq-dates.l4).
@export By when must the National Board make its preliminary assessment of my complaint once the Queensland health ombudsman has referred it, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `the day it was received` IS A DATE @desc The date the referred complaint was received from the health ombudsman (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must the Board assess my referred complaint question` IS
    `the s 150 assessment deadline for a matter received on` `the day it was received`

@export Did the National Board make its preliminary assessment of my referred complaint in time under section 150(1) of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `the day it was received` IS A DATE @desc The date the referred complaint was received from the health ombudsman (ISO 8601)
      `the day it was assessed` IS A DATE @desc The date the Board made its preliminary assessment (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was my referred complaint assessed in time question` IS
    `the day it was assessed` `falls on or before` (`the s 150 assessment deadline for a matter received on` `the day it was received`)

-- s 151(1): "A National Board may decide to take no further action in relation to a referred matter if -- (a) the Board
-- reasonably believes the referred matter is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance; or (b) given
-- the amount of time that has elapsed ..., it is not practicable for the Board to investigate or otherwise deal with the
-- referred matter; or (c) the person ... has not been, or is no longer, registered in a health profession for which the
-- Board is established and it is not in the public interest ...; or (d) the subject matter ... has already been dealt
-- with adequately by the Board; or (e) the subject matter of the notification -- (i) is being dealt with, or has already
-- been dealt with, by another entity; or (ii) has been referred by the Board under section 150 or 150C to another entity
-- ...; or (f) the health practitioner ... has taken appropriate steps to remedy the matter ... and the Board reasonably
-- believes no further action is required". (2): a later "pattern of conduct". (3): "If a National Board decides to take
-- no further action in relation to a referred matter, it must give written notice of the decision to the complainant
-- (if any) for the matter." (4): the notice "must state ... (b) the reason".
DECLARE `Can the Board take no further action` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Board may decide to take no further action (s 151(1)); if it does, it must tell you in writing, with its reason (s 151(3), (4)), and it may still take your complaint into account later as part of a pattern of conduct (s 151(2))`
    `no: section 151 lets the Board take no further action only if one of its grounds holds (s 151(1)(a)-(f))`

@export Can the National Board decide to take no further action on my complaint referred by the Queensland health ombudsman, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `frivolous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board reasonably believes your complaint is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 151(1)(a))
      `too old` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of the time since it happened, it is not practicable for the Board to investigate or deal with it (s 151(1)(b))
      `not registered with this Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner has not been, or is no longer, registered in a profession for this Board (s 151(1)(c))
      `not in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a practitioner not (or no longer) registered with this Board: whether it is not in the public interest for the Board to deal with it (s 151(1)(c))
      `already dealt with by the Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board has already dealt adequately with the same subject matter (s 151(1)(d))
      `dealt with or referred elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another body (for example a court, the police or a hospital) is dealing or has dealt with it, or the Board referred it to another Board or body under s 150 or s 150C (s 151(1)(e))
      `remedied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner has taken appropriate steps to remedy the matter (s 151(1)(f))
      `no more needed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a remedied matter: whether the Board reasonably believes no further action is required (s 151(1)(f))
GIVETH A `Can the Board take no further action`
DECIDE `Can the Board take no further action question` IS
    IF     `frivolous`
        OR `too old`
        OR (`not registered with this Board` AND `not in the public interest`)
        OR `already dealt with by the Board`
        OR `dealt with or referred elsewhere`
        OR (`remedied` AND `no more needed`)
    THEN `yes: the Board may decide to take no further action (s 151(1)); if it does, it must tell you in writing, with its reason (s 151(3), (4)), and it may still take your complaint into account later as part of a pattern of conduct (s 151(2))`
    ELSE `no: section 151 lets the Board take no further action only if one of its grounds holds (s 151(1)(a)-(f))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 159A-192 -- what I am told later, and taking part in a panel hearing`
-- Arises only once the National Board or a panel has decided something on a referred complaint.

-- s 159A(2) (immediate action), s 167A(2) (decision after investigation), s 177A(2) (decision after an assessor's
-- report): the National Board "may inform the complainant who made the complaint of the decision and the reasons for the
-- decision". s 161(3): "the National Board must, at not less than 3 monthly intervals, give the written notice of the
-- progress of the investigation to -- ... (b) if the investigation relates to a complaint made about the registered
-- health practitioner or student, the complainant." s 180(1): "As soon as practicable after making a decision under
-- section 179(2), the National Board must give written notice of the decision to -- ... (b) if the decision was the
-- result of a complaint, the complainant." (2): "may also include the reasons". s 192(2): "The National Board must,
-- within 30 days after the panel makes its decision, give written notice of the decision to -- ... (b) if the hearing
-- related to a complaint, the complainant." (4): "may also include the reasons".
DECLARE `What was decided` IS ONE OF
    `the Board decided to investigate (s 160)`
    `the Board took immediate action (for example suspended the practitioner or imposed conditions) (s 156)`
    `the Board decided what to do after an investigation (s 167)`
    `the Board decided what to do after a health or performance assessment report (s 177)`
    `the Board took action after the show cause process (caution, undertaking, conditions, reprimand and the like) (s 179(2))`
    `a health panel or performance and professional standards panel decided the matter (s 191 or s 191A)`

DECLARE `What I must be told` IS ONE OF
    `the Board must give you written notice of the progress of the investigation at not less than 3 monthly intervals (s 161(3)(b)); the exceptions in s 161(4) cover only notices to the practitioner or student`
    `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 159A(2))`
    `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 167A(2))`
    `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 177A(2))`
    `the Board must give you written notice of the decision as soon as practicable; it may also give the reasons (s 180(1)(b), (2))`
    `the Board must give you written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after the panel makes it; it may also give the reasons (s 192(2)(b), (4))`

@export What must the National Board tell me, as the complainant, about a decision on my complaint under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `what was decided` IS A `What was decided` @desc The stage at which the Board or a panel decided something on your complaint
GIVETH A `What I must be told`
DECIDE `What must I be told about the decision question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board decided to investigate (s 160)`
            THEN `the Board must give you written notice of the progress of the investigation at not less than 3 monthly intervals (s 161(3)(b)); the exceptions in s 161(4) cover only notices to the practitioner or student`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board took immediate action (for example suspended the practitioner or imposed conditions) (s 156)`
            THEN `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 159A(2))`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board decided what to do after an investigation (s 167)`
            THEN `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 167A(2))`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board decided what to do after a health or performance assessment report (s 177)`
            THEN `the Board may tell you of the decision and its reasons, but need not (s 177A(2))`
        IF `what was decided` EQUALS `the Board took action after the show cause process (caution, undertaking, conditions, reprimand and the like) (s 179(2))`
            THEN `the Board must give you written notice of the decision as soon as practicable; it may also give the reasons (s 180(1)(b), (2))`
        OTHERWISE `the Board must give you written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after the panel makes it; it may also give the reasons (s 192(2)(b), (4))`

@export By when must the National Board next tell me how its investigation of my complaint is going, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `the day of the last notice` IS A DATE @desc The date the Board decided to investigate, or the date of its last progress notice to you if it has sent one (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I next hear of the investigation question` IS
    `the last day of 3 months after` `the day of the last notice`

@export By when must the National Board tell me of a panel's decision on my complaint under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `the day the panel decided` IS A DATE @desc The date the health panel or performance and professional standards panel made its decision (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I be told of the panel decision question` IS
    `the s 192 notice deadline for a panel decision on` `the day the panel decided`

-- s 187: "If a matter the subject of a hearing before a panel relates to a complaint, the complainant may, with the leave
-- of the panel, make a submission to the panel about the matter." s 189: "A hearing before a panel is not open to the
-- public."
DECLARE `Can I make a submission to the panel` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as the complainant you may make a submission to the panel about the matter, with the panel's leave (s 187); the hearing is not open to the public (s 189)`
    `not without the panel's leave: a complainant may make a submission only with the leave of the panel (s 187)`
    `no: section 187 lets the complainant make a submission only where the matter relates to the complaint`

@export Can I make a submission to the panel hearing about my complaint under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `the hearing is about my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter before the panel relates to the complaint you made
      `the panel gave me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the panel has given you permission (leave) to make a submission
GIVETH A `Can I make a submission to the panel`
DECIDE `Can I make a submission to the panel question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the hearing is about my complaint`)
            THEN `no: section 187 lets the complainant make a submission only where the matter relates to the complaint`
        IF `the panel gave me leave`
            THEN `yes: as the complainant you may make a submission to the panel about the matter, with the panel's leave (s 187); the hearing is not open to the public (s 189)`
        OTHERWISE `not without the panel's leave: a complainant may make a submission only with the leave of the panel (s 187)`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 193-193C, and ss 6 and 9 of the Act -- serious matters, the health ombudsman and QCAT`
-- Arises only where the National Board is dealing with a matter and it looks like professional misconduct or a ground
-- for suspending or cancelling registration.

-- s 193(1): "The National Board must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable after -- (a) the National Board
-- forms a reasonable belief, based on a complaint or for any other reason, that -- (i) a registered health practitioner
-- has behaved in a way that constitutes professional misconduct; or (ii) there is another ground for suspending,
-- cancelling or withdrawing a registered health practitioner's registration; or (b) a panel notifies the National Board
-- of the panel's belief under section 190(2)." (1A): "Subsection (1)(a) does not apply if the National Board is satisfied
-- the health ombudsman is aware of the matter". (2): "On receiving the notification, the health ombudsman must ask the
-- National Board to -- (a) refer the matter to the health ombudsman; or (b) continue to deal with the matter under the
-- National Law." s 193A(1): the Board "must refer" if asked. s 193B(2): if asked to continue, the Board "may refer a
-- matter ... to a responsible tribunal". s 193B(4): "If the matter relates to a complaint, the referral must include the
-- details of the complainant." s 193C(1): the Board may decide not to refer if "there is no public interest in the
-- matter being heard by a responsible tribunal".
DECLARE `Must the health ombudsman be told` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the National Board must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable (s 193(1)); the health ombudsman must then ask the Board either to refer the matter to it, to be dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (ss 193(2)(a), 193A(1)), or to continue under this Law, when the Board may refer it to a responsible tribunal (ss 193(2)(b), 193B(2)) unless it decides there is no public interest in a hearing (s 193C); a referral about your complaint must include your details (s 193B(4))`
    `no: the Board need not notify the health ombudsman on its own belief, because it is satisfied the health ombudsman already knows of the matter (s 193(1A)), for example because the health ombudsman referred it`
    `no: s 193(1) arises only after the Board forms a reasonable belief of professional misconduct or another ground for suspension, cancellation or withdrawal, or a panel tells the Board it believes so (s 190(2))`

@export Must the National Board tell the Queensland health ombudsman about a serious matter arising from my complaint, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `the Board believes it is serious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board has formed a reasonable belief that the practitioner behaved in a way that is professional misconduct, or that there is another ground to suspend, cancel or withdraw their registration
      `a panel told the Board` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a panel hearing the matter has notified the Board that it believes the evidence shows professional misconduct or another ground for suspension, cancellation or withdrawal (s 190(2))
      `the ombudsman already knows` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board is satisfied the health ombudsman is already aware of the matter (for example because the health ombudsman referred it)
GIVETH A `Must the health ombudsman be told`
DECIDE `Must the Board tell the health ombudsman question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `a panel told the Board`
            THEN `yes: the National Board must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable (s 193(1)); the health ombudsman must then ask the Board either to refer the matter to it, to be dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (ss 193(2)(a), 193A(1)), or to continue under this Law, when the Board may refer it to a responsible tribunal (ss 193(2)(b), 193B(2)) unless it decides there is no public interest in a hearing (s 193C); a referral about your complaint must include your details (s 193B(4))`
        IF NOT (`the Board believes it is serious`)
            THEN `no: s 193(1) arises only after the Board forms a reasonable belief of professional misconduct or another ground for suspension, cancellation or withdrawal, or a panel tells the Board it believes so (s 190(2))`
        IF `the ombudsman already knows`
            THEN `no: the Board need not notify the health ombudsman on its own belief, because it is satisfied the health ombudsman already knows of the matter (s 193(1A)), for example because the health ombudsman referred it`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the National Board must notify the health ombudsman as soon as practicable (s 193(1)); the health ombudsman must then ask the Board either to refer the matter to it, to be dealt with under the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 (ss 193(2)(a), 193A(1)), or to continue under this Law, when the Board may refer it to a responsible tribunal (ss 193(2)(b), 193B(2)) unless it decides there is no public interest in a hearing (s 193C); a referral about your complaint must include your details (s 193B(4))`

-- s 193B(3): "The National Board must -- (a) refer the matter to -- (i) the responsible tribunal for the participating
-- jurisdiction in which the behaviour the subject of the matter occurred; or (ii) if the behaviour occurred in more than
-- one jurisdiction, the responsible tribunal for the participating jurisdiction in which the practitioner's principal
-- place of practice is located". s 6 of the Act: "QCAT is declared to be the responsible tribunal for this
-- jurisdiction". s 9 of the Act: an appeal to QCAT is "a review of the decision as provided under the QCAT Act".
DECLARE `Where the behaviour happened` IS ONE OF
    `only in Queensland`
    `only in one other participating jurisdiction (another State or Territory)`
    `in more than one jurisdiction`

DECLARE `Which tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `QCAT, the responsible tribunal for Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(i); s 6 of the Act)`
    `the responsible tribunal of the other State or Territory where the behaviour occurred (s 193B(3)(a)(i))`
    `QCAT, because the behaviour occurred in more than one jurisdiction and the practitioner's principal place of practice is in Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(ii); s 6 of the Act)`
    `the responsible tribunal of the State or Territory of the practitioner's principal place of practice, because the behaviour occurred in more than one jurisdiction (s 193B(3)(a)(ii))`

@export If the National Board refers my complaint to a tribunal, which tribunal must hear it under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the behaviour happened` @desc Where the behaviour your complaint is about happened
      `principal place of practice in Queensland` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner's principal place of practice (the address they declared as where they mainly practise) is in Queensland
GIVETH A `Which tribunal`
DECIDE `Which tribunal hears a Board referral question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `where it happened` EQUALS `only in Queensland`
            THEN `QCAT, the responsible tribunal for Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(i); s 6 of the Act)`
        IF `where it happened` EQUALS `only in one other participating jurisdiction (another State or Territory)`
            THEN `the responsible tribunal of the other State or Territory where the behaviour occurred (s 193B(3)(a)(i))`
        IF `principal place of practice in Queensland`
            THEN `QCAT, because the behaviour occurred in more than one jurisdiction and the practitioner's principal place of practice is in Queensland (s 193B(3)(a)(ii); s 6 of the Act)`
        OTHERWISE `the responsible tribunal of the State or Territory of the practitioner's principal place of practice, because the behaviour occurred in more than one jurisdiction (s 193B(3)(a)(ii))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `s 129 -- did the practitioner who treated me have to be insured`

-- s 129(1): "A registered health practitioner must not practise the health profession in which the practitioner is
-- registered unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force in relation to the
-- practitioner's practice of the profession." (4): "A contravention of subsection (1) ... does not constitute an offence
-- but may constitute behaviour for which health, conduct or performance action may be taken." (5): "registered health
-- practitioner does not include a registered health practitioner who holds non-practising registration in the
-- profession." The registration standard that gives "appropriate" its content is not deposited; compliance is asked.
DECLARE `Their registration type` IS ONE OF
    `practising registration (general, specialist, provisional or limited)`
    `non-practising registration`
    `student registration`
    `not registered`

DECLARE `Did they have to be insured` IS ONE OF
    `yes: they must not practise unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force (s 129(1)); they had them`
    `yes, and they were not: practising without appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements breaches s 129(1); it is not an offence but may lead to health, conduct or performance action (s 129(4)), and you may notify the health ombudsman on that ground (s 144(1)(e))`
    `no: s 129 does not apply while the practitioner is not practising the profession`
    `no: s 129 does not apply to non-practising registration (s 129(5))`
    `no: s 129 applies to a registered health practitioner, which by definition excludes registration as a student (s 5)`
    `no: s 129 applies only to a registered health practitioner (s 5)`

@export Did the practitioner who treated me have to have professional indemnity insurance under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `their registration` IS A `Their registration type` @desc The practitioner's registration when they treated you, as the public register shows it
      `they were practising` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner was practising their health profession (for example treating you) at the time
      `they had compliant cover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether professional indemnity insurance arrangements meeting their Board's registration standard were in force for their practice then (you may not know; answer as you believe)
GIVETH A `Did they have to be insured`
DECIDE `Did the practitioner have to be insured question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `not registered`
            THEN `no: s 129 applies only to a registered health practitioner (s 5)`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `student registration`
            THEN `no: s 129 applies to a registered health practitioner, which by definition excludes registration as a student (s 5)`
        IF `their registration` EQUALS `non-practising registration`
            THEN `no: s 129 does not apply to non-practising registration (s 129(5))`
        IF NOT (`they were practising`)
            THEN `no: s 129 does not apply while the practitioner is not practising the profession`
        IF `they had compliant cover`
            THEN `yes: they must not practise unless appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements are in force (s 129(1)); they had them`
        OTHERWISE `yes, and they were not: practising without appropriate professional indemnity insurance arrangements breaches s 129(1); it is not an offence but may lead to health, conduct or performance action (s 129(4)), and you may notify the health ombudsman on that ground (s 144(1)(e))`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `s 237, and s 56AA of the Act -- my protections`

-- s 237(1): "This section applies to a person who, in good faith -- (a) makes a notification under this Law; or (b) gives
-- information in the course of an investigation or for another purpose under this Law to a person exercising functions
-- under this Law." (2): "The person is not liable, civilly, criminally or under an administrative process, for giving
-- the information." (3): no breach of "professional etiquette or ethics", and "no liability for defamation". (4): "The
-- protection ... extends to -- (a) a person who, in good faith, provided the person with any information on the basis of
-- which the notification was made ...; and (b) a person who, in good faith, was otherwise concerned in the making of the
-- notification".
DECLARE `What I did` IS ONE OF
    `made a notification (complaint) to the health ombudsman about a registered health practitioner or student`
    `gave information in an investigation, or for another purpose under this Law, to a person exercising functions under it`
    `gave the person who notified the information the notification was based on, or was otherwise concerned in making it`

DECLARE `Am I protected` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for it, it is not a breach of professional etiquette or ethics, and no liability for defamation is incurred (s 237(1)-(3))`
    `yes: the protection extends to a person who, in good faith, gave the notifier the information or was otherwise concerned in the notification (s 237(4))`
    `no: section 237 protects only a person acting in good faith (s 237(1), (4))`

@export Am I protected from being sued or prosecuted for complaining about a health practitioner, or giving information, under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland)?
GIVEN `what I did` IS A `What I did` @desc What you did
      `in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you acted in good faith (honestly, not for an improper purpose); a court decides this if it is disputed
GIVETH A `Am I protected`
DECIDE `Am I protected from liability question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`in good faith`)
            THEN `no: section 237 protects only a person acting in good faith (s 237(1), (4))`
        IF `what I did` EQUALS `gave the person who notified the information the notification was based on, or was otherwise concerned in making it`
            THEN `yes: the protection extends to a person who, in good faith, gave the notifier the information or was otherwise concerned in the notification (s 237(4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for it, it is not a breach of professional etiquette or ethics, and no liability for defamation is incurred (s 237(1)-(3))`

-- s 56AA of the Act: "National Law provisions, sections 237A and 237B -- omit." s 59: "National Law provisions, section
-- 329 -- omit." The National Law (Queensland) prints "Sections 237A and 237B are not applicable in Queensland."
-- Elsewhere s 237A makes a reprisal against a notifier an offence and s 237B voids a non-disclosure agreement to the
-- extent it stops a notification. In Queensland the Health Ombudsman Act 2013 ss 261-263 (reprisals) and s 263A
-- (non-disclosure agreements) are where to look; they are named, not encoded.
DECLARE `Which protection` IS ONE OF
    `protection from a reprisal (for example being sacked or threatened) for complaining or giving information`
    `protection from a confidentiality (non-disclosure) clause that would stop me complaining`

DECLARE `Does this Law protect me` IS ONE OF
    `no: section 237A (reprisals) is omitted from the National Law (Queensland) by s 56AA of the Act; in Queensland, reprisals are dealt with by the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, ss 261-263 (an offence, and a tort giving damages), not encoded here`
    `no: section 237B (non-disclosure agreements) is omitted from the National Law (Queensland) by s 56AA of the Act, and s 329 by s 59; in Queensland, non-disclosure agreements are dealt with by the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, s 263A, not encoded here`

@export Does the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (Queensland) itself protect me from a reprisal, or from a confidentiality clause, for complaining about a health practitioner?
GIVEN `which protection` IS A `Which protection` @desc The protection you are asking about
GIVETH A `Does this Law protect me`
DECIDE `Does the National Law (Queensland) give this protection question` IS
    IF `which protection` EQUALS `protection from a reprisal (for example being sacked or threatened) for complaining or giving information`
    THEN `no: section 237A (reprisals) is omitted from the National Law (Queensland) by s 56AA of the Act; in Queensland, reprisals are dealt with by the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, ss 261-263 (an offence, and a tort giving damages), not encoded here`
    ELSE `no: section 237B (non-disclosure agreements) is omitted from the National Law (Queensland) by s 56AA of the Act, and s 329 by s 59; in Queensland, non-disclosure agreements are dealt with by the Health Ombudsman Act 2013, s 263A, not encoded here`

-- ======================================================================================================================
§§ `Regulative rules -- the deadlines the Law sets the National Board`

-- The two periods the Law states in days for the complainant's matter, each written once here and used by both the seat
-- questions and the regulative rules below. Each is reckoned by Schedule 7 cl 31(2), (3) (see hprnlq-dates.l4).
-- s 150(1): "within 60 days after receipt of a referred matter under section 148".
GIVEN received IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 150 assessment deadline for a matter received on` received MEANS `the last day of` 60 `days after` received

-- s 192(2)(b): "within 30 days after the panel makes its decision".
GIVEN decided IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 192 notice deadline for a panel decision on` decided MEANS `the last day of` 30 `days after` decided

DECLARE `National Law (Queensland) party` IS ONE OF
    `the National Board`

DECLARE `National Law (Queensland) action` IS ONE OF
    `conduct the preliminary assessment of the referred matter (s 150(1))`
    `give the complainant written notice of the panel's decision (s 192(2)(b))`

-- s 150(1): the Board MUST conduct the preliminary assessment within 60 days after receipt. The Law states no
-- consequence of a late assessment; the LEST records the breach only.
GIVEN received IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `National Law (Queensland) party` `National Law (Queensland) action`
`the s 150 assessment duty for a matter received on` received MEANS
    PARTY `the National Board`
    MUST  `conduct the preliminary assessment of the referred matter (s 150(1))`
    WITHIN `days from` received `to` (`the s 150 assessment deadline for a matter received on` received)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the National Board` BECAUSE "s 150(1): no preliminary assessment within 60 days after receipt of the referred matter"

-- s 192(2)(b): the Board MUST give the complainant written notice of the panel's decision within 30 days after it.
GIVEN decided IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `National Law (Queensland) party` `National Law (Queensland) action`
`the s 192 notice duty for a panel decision on` decided MEANS
    PARTY `the National Board`
    MUST  `give the complainant written notice of the panel's decision (s 192(2)(b))`
    WITHIN `days from` decided `to` (`the s 192 notice deadline for a panel decision on` decided)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the National Board` BECAUSE "s 192(2)(b): the complainant was not told of the panel's decision within 30 days"
